function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/3c2cbbc268e070fa6a9ad887d06d94e73db19a70337bff2f9f11d67973d02898.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-122790/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122790"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122834/selection/f7324534d6dae3e97cf19d85f8c7ab8a9ca12ff9b1f9adbac1e0085d1318c45f" :answer/id #uuid "d3de245e-f5b6-53b1-9617-a7aff2e2299d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/08a6451e354eaaad23ef233a8c43d16512a2ed33257cd9e9b1257ef5eaaf558b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122834/selection/a4b2114abc92761edefb095d7c246e47b80d902b6eb786c10eab1522dd8b358b" :answer/id #uuid "a95929db-422e-51fe-bf42-d73a79fb55f4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/c5c0cc02a032916c8f9216673f23f3db81535828b14e51889942ffaf452ac3cb.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122834/selection/6f74ee5b8a6434ed26404db0cd24b8ef08ea4097a1e4cbd18faa1b4df572895f" :answer/id #uuid "bf4ace8e-2ef2-5b45-9b33-575e89bf9e8e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/57f5858f1ccae96a169ee0aac3f106bd44b3de8282412389f4eb8d3ad8b66af9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122834/selection/5bba067198bc35746499fa45972634dce9acccf89eb7accc71c77a55913a5b76" :answer/id #uuid "23fc4ad8-5a2a-57f4-b15e-7970bebec82d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/e76b318c2f347bc59746ea0dc88c838ad46694724563867a0ee94318c826d887.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122834/selection/6526a11bddc20d1b3b0d111ff4bd12574d674096c34a209c5ada8b0ff8219689" :answer/id #uuid "7a00d1e7-85b4-568b-964d-d7f749fac776" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122834/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0456780-40a1-514d-9f84-3c653e868a7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122834/selection/6526a11bddc20d1b3b0d111ff4bd12574d674096c34a209c5ada8b0ff8219689" :answer-field/choices ["q-122834/selection/f7324534d6dae3e97cf19d85f8c7ab8a9ca12ff9b1f9adbac1e0085d1318c45f" "q-122834/selection/a4b2114abc92761edefb095d7c246e47b80d902b6eb786c10eab1522dd8b358b" "q-122834/selection/6f74ee5b8a6434ed26404db0cd24b8ef08ea4097a1e4cbd18faa1b4df572895f" "q-122834/selection/5bba067198bc35746499fa45972634dce9acccf89eb7accc71c77a55913a5b76" "q-122834/selection/6526a11bddc20d1b3b0d111ff4bd12574d674096c34a209c5ada8b0ff8219689"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122834" :question/id #uuid "ec7c19a9-6da7-55d6-b075-dc647a76929b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122834" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=-1,$ which gives the vertical hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the pair of lines $y=\\pm x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2,$ which gives the horizontal hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the right half-plane $x>0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the left half-plane $x<0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/037f46d3fdd190749c2aefe51222475fdd337a7b3ce53e6809048e3491a31a47.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044724/assets/346e6bb41b8dd3b353dfedbc55eb25690396d5492d18199ba99a12523452db0a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-122834/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "364d2c9a-6e9a-57f8-aa57-c10ee223061b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122834"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122838/selection/98e5451160da1676b15532db95358ebd07a69cc6f37a68f36ac86b8cead439ce" :answer/id #uuid "5505ac41-3737-5fb1-b3fc-614d74c56cef" :answer/type :answer.type/ima